<p>I'm <a href="https://bradgessler.com" rel="author">Brad Gessler</a>. I've been
building on the web for a long time, and lately I've been building a lot of it
with agents. Somewhere in there I noticed two things at once.</p>

<p>The first is that the browser is astonishingly capable. It validates forms,
traps focus in dialogs, transitions between pages, fills in login codes from
your text messages, and reads a document out loud to someone who can't see it.
None of that costs you anything. It's already there.</p>

<p>The second is that we keep paying to replace it. Every year a new layer of
abstraction goes between the person writing the page and the thing that renders
it, and every layer makes it a little easier to forget what the medium can do
on its own. So we end up with a six-box login code that breaks paste, a modal
inside a modal, and a twenty-minute deploy to fix a typo. Not because anyone
decided to. Because nobody checked.</p>

<p>Keeping it simple turns out to be the better deal on almost every axis that
matters. It's cheaper to build and cheaper to run. It's faster for the person
on the other end. It's accessible by default instead of by heroics. And, more
and more, it's what an agent can actually understand: plain hypermedia is
legible to a model in a way a pile of client-side state never will be.</p>

<p>So I made this as a fun way for people, and for agents, to learn how
hypermedia works and why it's worth using directly. Each article takes one
thing we habitually over-build, shows it breaking, and shows the smaller thing
that already worked. Short enough to check yourself. Loud enough to remember.</p>

<p class="sting">The browser already does that.</p>

<p>It's open. <a href="https://github.com/bradgessler/hypermedia.lol/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md">Send
a pull request</a>. One file, markdown or HTML, and the only rule is no
JavaScript for the site's own UI. There's also a
<a href="/llms.txt">llms.txt</a>, written for the agents.</p>
